{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the explosive rise of facebook and the bitter, complex relationships that fueled its creation. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the movie is less a biopic and more a psychological drama about ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation in the digital age. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached—a genius whose drive for validation and control blinds him to the human consequences of his actions.\n\nthe screenplay, written by aaron sorkin, is a tour de force of rapid-fire dialogue and layered storytelling. every line feels deliberate, every exchange charged with tension and subtext. the narrative unfolds through a dual courtroom framework, with the winklevoss twins and divya narendra suing zuckerberg for stealing their idea, and the co-founder sean parker facing charges of intellectual theft from his former partner, eduardo saverin. these legal proceedings serve as framing devices, but the real drama lies in the unraveling of friendships and the erosion of trust.\n\njoseph gordon-levitt shines as the loyal but increasingly sidelined saverin, embodying the moral center of the story. his quiet devastation as he watches his friendship with zuckerberg disintegrate is heartbreaking. and then there’s justin timberlake as sean parker—charismatic, chaotic, and intoxicatingly dangerous. his performance is electric, capturing the allure and toxicity of the tech bro archetype before the term even became common.\n\nthe film’s aesthetic is cold and sleek, mirroring the sterile world of code and corporate law. trent reznor and atticus ross’s haunting, minimalist score pulses like a heartbeat beneath the digital surface, adding emotional weight to scenes that might otherwise feel clinical. the cinematography is precise, with tight close-ups and stark lighting that emphasize isolation even in crowded rooms.\n\nwhat makes the social network so compelling is its refusal to offer easy answers. it doesn’t glorify zuckerberg nor vilify him outright. instead, it presents him as a product of his time—a young man who built a global empire by turning human connection into a data stream. the movie asks uncomfortable questions about ownership, ethics, and the price of success in the internet era.\n\nmore than a decade after its release, the social network remains startlingly relevant. it predicted the cultural and political fallout of social media long before most people understood its power. it’s not just a movie about facebook—it’s a mirror held up to the digital world we now inhabit. brilliant, unsettling, and endlessly watchable, the social network is a modern classic.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.37157994508743286}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009815642843022943}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0058195204474031925}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00013195598148740828}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -1.2010457515716553}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.597391747054644e-05}, {"token": " explosive", "logprob": -1.7369593381881714}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.10027764737606049}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.0411134250462055e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00035422726068645716}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.25446972250938416}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.2053474822314456e-05}, {"token": " bitter", "logprob": -1.5473005771636963}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.4